  "output": "## Performance Analysis\n\nI read through the data-access layer (`lib/server/*`), the schema, the client components in the review queue, and the export path. Headline finding: **this codebase is unusually disciplined about the server side already** — the comments in `lib/server/conflicts.ts` and `lib/server/entities.ts` explicitly call out \"not a query per row\" and batch accordingly. So there's no classic N+1 to fix. The real opportunities are on the React re-render side, in caching config-like tables, and a couple of O(n²) UI computations.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found, but worth confirming why\n\n`getQueuePage` (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:172`) fetches rows, counts, and child-counts as **3 queries total for a whole page**, not per-row:\n\n```ts\nconst [rows, counts, total] = await Promise.all([...]);\nconst childCounts = await countChildrenByParent(run.id, rows.flatMap(r => [r.aExternalId, r.bExternalId]));\n```\n\nSame pattern in `lib/server/export.ts` (`loadOrphans` batches `countChildrenByParent` once for the whole export, not per row). This is correct and deliberate — no action needed here, just flagging that I checked and it holds up.\n\nOne micro-nit, not a query issue: `countSourcesBySide()` (`lib/server/sources.ts:24`) does three separate `.filter()` passes over the same array:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\nTrivial at real data volumes (sources list is small), but a single `reduce` is cheaper and reads just as clearly:\n```ts\nreturn all.reduce(\n  (acc, s) => {\n    if (s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\") acc.crm++;\n    else if (s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\") acc.warehouse++;\n    else if (s.kind === \"synthetic\") acc.synthetic++;\n    return acc;\n  },\n  { crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 },\n);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders — real issue in the conflict queue\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`) holds `selectedIndex` in state and passes rows down to `ConflictDenseTable` / `ConflictFocus` / the ledger `.map`. **None of the row components (`ConflictDiffRow`, `ListRow`, the dense-table row) are wrapped in `React.memo`**, and the callback passed to each row is not referentially stable:\n\n```ts\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit =>\n    (action: ReviewAction) => submit(conflictId, action),   // new closure every call\n  [submit],\n);\n```\n\n`submitFor` itself is memoized, but **calling** it (`submitFor(row.conflictId)`) allocates a brand-new function every render, for every row. Combined with no `memo()` on the row components, pressing `j`/`k` to move the keyboard selection re-renders every row on the page (up to 100 rows in dense view, each with a diff computation, severity mark, rationale block) even though only the previously-selected and newly-selected rows actually changed visually.\n\nFix — memoize the row components and give them stable per-row submit handlers:\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ row, submit, ... }) {\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — one stable callback per row via a ref map, or pass conflictId + submit down\n// and let the leaf call submit(conflictId, action) directly instead of currying per-row:\n<ConflictDiffRow\n  key={row.conflictId}\n  row={row}\n  onReview={submit}          // stable, from useCallback([submit])\n  conflictId={row.conflictId}\n  ...\n/>\n```\nthen inside the row: `submit(conflictId, action)` instead of a pre-curried closure. That removes the per-row allocation and lets `React.memo` actually short-circuit re-renders when `selectedIndex` changes elsewhere.\n\nSame shape applies to `ConflictDenseTable` (`components/conflicts/conflict-dense-table.tsx:65`) and `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow` — both re-derive `submit(row.conflictId)` inline in the `.map`.\n\nSecondary, compounding cost: `DiffValue` (`components/conflicts/diff-value.tsx`) runs `diffSegments(value, against)` — an O(n) char-walk — on every render of every visible value cell. Cheap per call, but it re-runs for the whole page on every keystroke because of the re-render fan-out above. Once the rows are memoized this stops mattering; it's not worth a separate `useMemo` on its own.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n`sources` and `rulesets` are genuinely low-churn, config-like tables (a new source is a rare admin action; a ruleset is versioned and appended-to, not edited). Both are read via `connection()`, which forces the whole page into fully dynamic rendering and re-queries Postgres on **every** request:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nexport async function listSources(): Promise<Source[]> {\n  await connection();  // opts out of prerendering for every caller\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThis is called from `new-run-form.tsx`'s page, `sources/page.tsx`, and `ruleset-editor.tsx`'s page — every navigation re-hits the DB for a table that might change once a week. Worth wrapping in `unstable_cache` with a tag, invalidated on write:\n\n```ts\nimport { unstable_cache, revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources-list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n\n// in createSource, after the insert:\nrevalidateTag(\"sources\");\n```\nSame pattern for `listRulesets` / `getActiveRuleset` / `getRuleset` in `lib/server/rulesets.ts`, tagged `\"rulesets\"` and invalidated in `createRulesetVersion`. This turns N DB round-trips per hour into effectively 0 between writes, with correctness preserved via tag invalidation (no staleness risk, since the write path is the only mutator and it's in the same codebase).\n\nI'd leave `entities`/`conflicts`/`matches`/`runs` reads alone — those are per-run, request-scoped data where staleness would be a real bug, and `connection()` there is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found; one scaling concern\n\nI checked every `useEffect` with a subscription/timer (`RunLive`'s `setInterval` in `Elapsed`, `LogoLoop`'s `ResizeObserver`, `TextType`'s `setTimeout`, the `keydown` listener in `ConflictQueue`) — **all have correct cleanup functions**. TanStack Query's `QueryClient` is created once per `QueryProvider` mount via `useState`, scoped to the page that needs polling, and garbage-collected on unmount. No leaks.\n\nOne real memory-footprint concern, not a leak: `buildRunExport` (`lib/server/export.ts:122`) loads the full decision log and hierarchy into memory, then `GET /api/runs/[runId]/export` (`app/api/runs/[runId]/export/route.ts:60`) does `JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2)` and buffers the entire response as one string before sending:\n\n```ts\nreturn new Response(JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2), { ... });\n```\n\nFor a run with tens of thousands of conflicts this holds the whole serialized export in memory per concurrent export request. Since it's explicitly a v1 design (\"this file is the only thing that leaves the system\"), I wouldn't rewrite it preemptively — but if large runs become common, a streaming `ReadableStream` that writes CSV rows (or NDJSON) incrementally would cap memory to one row at a time instead of the whole payload.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n`RulesetEditor` (`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166`) does `O(n²)` work per render for what should be `O(n)`:\n\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);           // O(n) per row\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      key={rule.id}\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}      // another O(n) per row\n      ...\n```\n\nFor a ruleset with `n` rules this is `2n²` comparisons every render (every keystroke in any field, since state lives in the parent). Rule counts are small today, but it's needless — build an index map once:\n\n```tsx\nconst ruleIndex = new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i]));\nconst orderIndex = new Map(ordered.map((r, i) => [r.id, i]));\n\n{ordered.map((rule) => (\n  <RuleRow\n    key={rule.id}\n    rule={rule}\n    position={orderIndex.get(rule.id)! + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(ruleIndex.get(rule.id)!, patch)}\n    onRemove={rules.length > 1 ? () => removeRule(ruleIndex.get(rule.id)!) : undefined}\n    ...\n```\n\nAlso `orderRulesByEvaluation(rules)` re-sorts on every keystroke in the name/field/description inputs — harmless at current scale but a `useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules])` would make the cost proportional to actual rule changes rather than every render.\n\n---\n\n### Summary, ranked by actual impact\n\n| # | Issue | File | Fix effort |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Queue row re-render fan-out on keyboard nav | `conflict-queue.tsx`, `conflict-dense-table.tsx`, `conflict-focus.tsx` | Medium — memo + stable callback |\n| 2 | No caching on `sources`/`rulesets` reads | `lib/server/sources.ts`, `lib/server/rulesets.ts` | Small — `unstable_cache` + tag |\n| 3 | O(n²) `indexOf` in ruleset editor | `ruleset-editor.tsx` | Small — index map |\n| 4 | Export buffers full payload in memory | `app/api/runs/[runId]/export/route.ts` | Larger — only worth it if run sizes grow |\n| 5 | Triple-pass filter in `countSourcesBySide` | `lib/server/sources.ts` | Trivial, low value |\n\nItem 1 is the one I'd actually prioritize — it's the screen reviewers spend the most time in, and it's real, measurable re-render waste today, not a hypothetical scale problem.\n",
